# Copy to .env and fill. NEVER commit .env or real secrets.
#
# That first line is now true. It was not before (#417): every name here lacked the `GV_` prefix the
# application reads, `Settings` never read a `.env` file at all, and the port was 5432 where the local
# stack uses 5433. Following the instruction produced an application that would not start, and nothing
# noticed — so `tests/test_env_example.py` now reads this file and `app/config.py` together, and a
# renamed setting fails the build.
#
# **Only real settings may be uncommented here.** `Settings` sets `extra="forbid"`, so an unknown key in
# `.env` is refused outright — `Settings()` raises rather than ignoring it. That is why the values this
# project will need later but does not read yet are written as comments at the bottom rather than as
# live lines: an active `AWS_REGION` here would stop the API booting.
#
# Precedence, so a stale file cannot surprise you: a real environment variable wins over the same key in
# `.env`. CI and containers pass variables directly, so this file never overrides them.

# --- Required ---

# The application database. Port 5433, matching docker-compose.yml — deliberately not 5432, so it
# cannot collide with a PostgreSQL you already run and connect you to the wrong database in silence.
GV_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://gv:gv@localhost:5433/gv

# The engine client token. Not strictly required by `Settings` — it defaults to empty — but a worker
# refuses to start without it, so leaving it out gives you a process that exits 78 and a puzzle.
#
# Where the value comes from: run `make up`, then `make token` (#416). It prints the line to paste here
# and also writes `.hatchet-token`, which is gitignored. Tokens expire after 90 days; run it again.
GV_HATCHET_TOKEN=

# --- Optional: uncomment to override the default ---

# Which environment this is. It affects what /health reports — and it is also one of the two locks on
# the development identity below, which `app/auth/development.py` installs only when this says
# `development`. The default is already `development`, so a laptop needs this only to be explicit.
#GV_ENVIRONMENT=development

# The request-id header the API accepts and echoes.
#GV_REQUEST_ID_HEADER=X-Request-ID

# Namespace prefix for workflow names, so two environments can share one engine without one picking up
# the other's packages. Empty means no prefix.
#GV_HATCHET_NAMESPACE=

# How many package revisions may be processed at once. 1 on purpose: one 8 GB VM shares memory between
# rendering, OCR and PostgreSQL. Raising it should follow a measurement, not an assumption.
#GV_MAX_CONCURRENT_PACKAGES=1

# How many tasks one worker runs at once.
#GV_MAX_CONCURRENT_PAGE_TASKS=2

# How long the outbox dispatcher waits between polls. This is the visible wait between a package being
# accepted and its workflow starting, and somebody is watching.
#GV_OUTBOX_POLL_SECONDS=2.0

# How many outbox rows one pass may dispatch. Bounds both the transaction's lock time and how much work
# repeats if the process dies mid-pass.
#GV_OUTBOX_BATCH_LIMIT=100

# --- Read from the environment, and NOT from this file ---
#
# **Putting these in `.env` stops the application starting.** They are read by
# `app/auth/development.py` through `os.environ` directly, so they are not fields on `Settings` — and
# `extra="forbid"` means `Settings()` refuses an unknown key it finds in `.env`. Verified, not
# assumed: with both written into `.env`, `Settings()` raises `extra_forbidden` for each and the API
# never boots. `tests/test_env_example.py` would also fail the build if they were live keys here,
# because it checks every active key against a real setting.
#
# So export them for the process instead:
#
#   GV_DEV_PRINCIPAL="your name" \
#   GV_DEV_PROJECTS=<project-uuid> \
#   python scripts/dev_server.py
#
# `GV_DEV_PRINCIPAL` is the signed-in person's name; setting it at all is what asks for a development
# identity. `GV_DEV_PROJECTS` is a comma-separated list of project UUIDs that identity may see — the
# API authorises per project, so a principal with the wrong list gets 403 on findings that exist.
# `scripts/seed_demo.py` prints the project id it created, which is the one to use.
#
# Both are refused unless `GV_ENVIRONMENT` says `development`. Two locks rather than one: a deployment
# that forgets the environment still has to have exported the variable, and one that exports the
# variable still has to be calling itself development. No credential is checked, which is why.

# --- Not read by the application yet ---
#
# Kept as comments, not as live keys, for the reason at the top: `extra="forbid"` means an unknown key in
# `.env` stops the application starting. These become real settings when the stories that need them land,
# and each will get a field on `Settings` at that point.
#
# Object storage (C5 — S3 backend, #221, currently deferred):
#   AWS_REGION=us-east-1
#   S3_BUCKET=gv-v1-artifacts
#
# Bedrock (E2 — the VLM lane, ambiguous crops only). The verdict service must NOT receive these:
#   BEDROCK_MODEL_ID=amazon.nova-2-lite
#
# Traces (F2.1, #259). The tracer is built but no exporter is configured — where traces are shipped is an
# operations decision that has not been made:
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
#
# The engine's own database. Not an application setting: docker-compose.yml passes it to the engine
# container directly, and the engine — not Alembic — owns that schema:
#   HATCHET_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://gv:gv@localhost:5433/hatchet
